"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.3. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and pathological T3a stage with positive surgical margins, which are factors suggesting higher risk. Conversely, the seminal vesicles were not invaded, and lymphovascular invasion was absent. The absence of lymph node removal and the lack of documented nodal sampling widen the uncertainty regarding nodal status. The difference between the biopsy (Gleason 3+3, ISUP grade group 1) and the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 3+4, ISUP grade group 2) is noted. Postoperative PSA and its trajectory are critical missing pieces of information that would significantly revise this assessment. The current uncertainty regarding nodal status due to missing sampling also impacts the long-term outlook. The fixed estimated horizon is 80.15 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 52.10–108.20 months; its coverage has not been validated."
